comments.body:
Isn't marking this field as required in kubebuilder incorrect? The API docs on `Ports` say every port in the service should have an entry in the list, which means if a single port has an error they want to report, all ports must, even ones which don't have errors... so... the error field is optional.

I'd expect to resolve this by dropping the kubebuilder Required tag.

```
	// Ports is a list of records of service ports
	// If used, every port defined in the service should have an entry in it
	// +listType=atomic
	// +optional
	Ports []PortStatus `json:"ports,omitempty" protobuf:"bytes,4,rep,name=ports"`
```

Changing the structs would be akin to making a v2 of the client -- we still have to cope with v1.

> I would pose that the definition of optionality we want is based on the presence of the json key.

That's a possible interpretation, but I'm dubious.  We know that JSON and proto can serialize the same Go structs differently, ESPECIALLY around non-pointer structs.  Are we saying that the JSON serialization is the canonically correct one?  Aside from proto not conforming to that, we don't even use `omitzero` so I am not sure that definition works.  Let's look at a trivial example:

https://go.dev/play/p/pnprJcsm1qc

The result of JSON serializing a trivially empty Pod is:

```
{
    "spec": {
        "containers": null
    },
    "status": {}
}
```

There is no way for a Go client (structured) to produce a Pod where the `spec` and `status` keys are not  present in JSON.  Even if you had a client which can represent that, there is no way for us (in the apiserver) to detect the absence of those keys unless we 1) implement `omitzero`, 2) convert BACK to JSON to check presence/absence.  Many (most?) of our clients are go, so getting omitzero would bring new clients sort of back to par, but not fix exisiting clients.  On the server-side there are defaults, so `spec` will ALWAYS be present with non-zeroval fields.

If you look at these tags from a per-operation POV, "required" and "optional" can vary based on operation.  IMO, that is clearly NOT what the tags mean (or else we need to define which operation has which requiredness).

If you look at it from a schema POV, the tags describe the data - not the operational payload but the expected "final result" of every operation.  In that case, is it ever correct to have a Pod which doesn't have a `spec`?  You might send a serialized object which has no `spec` key, but either that will fail or the spec will get fixed server-side (e.g. sending to /status).  Doesn't that make the `spec` field required?
